WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:02.340 --> 00:00:07.410
Did you know the final book in the series,
Harry Potter and the Deathly Hallows holds

00:00:07.410 --> 00:00:14.690
the world record for the most copies sold
the day of release? On the 21st of July, 2007

00:00:14.690 --> 00:00:21.220
roughly 11 million books flew off the shelves
in just 24 hours and into the hands of young

00:00:21.220 --> 00:00:27.000
wannabe witches and wizards. But as soon as
J.K. Rowling had finished writing the last

00:00:27.000 --> 00:00:32.730
instalment of the series that’s earned her
hundreds of millions, even a billion by some

00:00:32.730 --> 00:00:38.670
estimates, she started on another book. The
Potter nerds among you will know that, in

00:00:38.670 --> 00:00:45.000
Deathly Hallows, Hermione is given a collection
of popular wizarding fairy tales titled The

00:00:45.000 --> 00:00:49.690
Tales of Beedle the Bard.
As a thank you to the key people that had

00:00:49.690 --> 00:00:55.560
made the Harry Potter series a reality, Rowling
decided to produce six copies of The Tales

00:00:55.560 --> 00:01:03.610
of Beedle the Bard, all handwritten and illustrated
by her and set with silver ornaments and semiprecious

00:01:03.610 --> 00:01:09.329
stones. A priceless way to say thank you,
you might think.

00:01:09.329 --> 00:01:16.229
Well actually, we can put a price on them,
because Rowling then changed her mind, opting

00:01:16.229 --> 00:01:22.779
to create a seventh copy, set with a moonstone,
to be sold at auction. The auction was to

00:01:22.779 --> 00:01:28.289
raise money for the children’s charity she’d
founded, and it took place on December 13th,

00:01:28.289 --> 00:01:34.679
2007, at Sotheby’s auction house in London.
It was expected to sell for around £50,000

00:01:34.679 --> 00:01:44.090
and it’s fair to say it surpassed expectations,
eventually being bought by Amazon for 1.95

00:01:44.090 --> 00:01:48.459
million pounds.
But even Potter-mania couldn’t propel this

00:01:48.459 --> 00:01:55.200
exclusive copy up into the echelons of the
most expensive books of all time. Near the

00:01:55.200 --> 00:02:01.659
top of the pile, just below William’s Shakespeare’s
First Folio and Leonardo Da Vinci’s scientific

00:02:01.659 --> 00:02:08.599
journal, lies the Gutenberg Bible, a copy
of which sold for nearly 8 million pounds

00:02:08.599 --> 00:02:15.170
in today’s money, back in 1978. The Bible
is considered the best-selling book of all

00:02:15.170 --> 00:02:22.870
time, with Guinness World Records estimating
in 1995 that over 5 billion copies had been

00:02:22.870 --> 00:02:29.569
sold worldwide – I mean it was alright,
I’ll admit the first part was quite gripping,

00:02:29.569 --> 00:02:35.359
I liked the plagues and mass genocide but
it all got a bit preachy in the second half.

00:02:35.359 --> 00:02:43.220
So, anyway, why is Gutenberg’s copy so expensive?
The Gutenberg Bible was one of, if not, the

00:02:43.220 --> 00:02:50.050
first book ever printed en masse, which is
a rather easy accolade to snap up when you’ve

00:02:50.050 --> 00:02:56.000
only gone and bloody invented the first ever
printing press. When goldsmith Johannes Gutenberg’s

00:02:56.000 --> 00:03:01.310
printing press hit the streets of Strasbourg
in 1440, where he was under political exile,

00:03:01.310 --> 00:03:07.250
he of course had the novel issue of needing
to create the world’s first font.

00:03:07.250 --> 00:03:12.930
Gutenberg used moveable type to print books
at far larger volumes than were previously

00:03:12.930 --> 00:03:18.930
possible, with the Gutenberg Bible being the
first to roll off the press. Reading started

00:03:18.930 --> 00:03:25.790
to shift from an exclusive pastime of the
elite to one more universally accessible – of

00:03:25.790 --> 00:03:30.890
course, every shit stinkin’ peasant being
able to read his own copy of the bible was

00:03:30.890 --> 00:03:37.780
the tinderbox for the protestant reformation
a handful of decades later. Gutenberg’s

00:03:37.780 --> 00:03:45.209
Textura (sometimes called Blackletter) can
be considered the world’s first font, and

00:03:45.209 --> 00:03:51.939
it was created to imitate a fairly intricate
handwritten style, in use since 1150. The

00:03:51.939 --> 00:03:58.599
scarcity of intact and complete copies of
Gutenberg’s Bible 600-years-on has led to

00:03:58.599 --> 00:04:07.170
an exorbitant price tag.While Gutenberg painstakingly
created his font by hand, these days, anyone

00:04:07.170 --> 00:04:13.359
can make their own font, and there are more
than half-a-million available worldwide. Today

00:04:13.359 --> 00:04:17.959
we’re spoilt for choice. You can spend a
good couple of hours choosing the perfect

00:04:17.959 --> 00:04:23.560
font from thousands for the front of your
‘Congratulations on fornicating’ … I

00:04:23.560 --> 00:04:29.850
mean ‘having a baby’ card. Choosing a
font was previously the domain of typesetters,

00:04:29.850 --> 00:04:36.120
but that all changed in 1984.
I’ll admit, our protagonist here is not

00:04:36.120 --> 00:04:42.030
the sort you’d expect to revolutionise the
way we write—he was a weed-smoking college

00:04:42.030 --> 00:04:48.340
dropout who’d taken a few calligraphy classes
after flunking his normal ones. But when he

00:04:48.340 --> 00:04:53.120
developed his initial model in a long line
of computers that were the first to offer

00:04:53.120 --> 00:04:59.430
their users a choice of typefaces, he loaded
it with some new fonts named after cities

00:04:59.430 --> 00:05:07.830
he loved—Toronto, San Francisco, London
and Geneva among others. One in particular

00:05:07.830 --> 00:05:15.169
would become very well known—Chicago was
the font for not just the computer menus,

00:05:15.169 --> 00:05:20.879
but those of the hugely successful portable
music players that followed.

00:05:20.879 --> 00:05:29.379
That student dropout was none other than poloneck
enthusiast Steve Jobs, and that computer was

00:05:29.379 --> 00:05:36.860
the very first Mac, and the music player the
iPod. While it was these devices that made

00:05:36.860 --> 00:05:44.800
him rich and famous, his biggest contribution
to mankind was giving us the ability to print

00:05:44.800 --> 00:05:52.060
out whatever we desired in any chosen font.
Be honest, we’ve all printed something in

00:05:52.060 --> 00:05:58.210
a horrific font – perhaps it was your list
of social mores for your bedroom door, including

00:05:58.210 --> 00:06:04.949
classics such as ‘no girls allowed’, ‘knock
before entering’ and ‘piss off mum, I’ve

00:06:04.949 --> 00:06:10.690
already got a dad and he’s not called Roberto’.
Indeed, abusing your family’s first printer

00:06:10.690 --> 00:06:17.691
with abhorrent typography is basically a 21st
century rite of passage. Thankfully, after

00:06:17.691 --> 00:06:22.990
that period of childhood experimentation,
most of us develop a slightly better eye for

00:06:22.990 --> 00:06:30.379
what works, and what induces ocular bleeding.
And indeed, certain fonts have surfaced as

00:06:30.379 --> 00:06:37.010
typographic A-listers.
Cooper Black is friendly and unthreatening—one

00:06:37.010 --> 00:06:42.270
might even call its letters pudgy. While it
enjoyed success in various uses, like the

00:06:42.270 --> 00:06:48.389
credits of British TV series Dad’s Army,
the Beach Boys album Pet Sounds, and of course,

00:06:48.389 --> 00:06:59.659
as the iconic font of choice for budget-so-long-as-you-haven’t-packed-your-grandma-airline
easyJet. Its use is even mandated in the company’s

00:06:59.659 --> 00:07:07.590
mission statement. But easyJet was only founded
in 1995, and Cooper Black was designed in

00:07:07.590 --> 00:07:13.150
the 1920s. So what attracted ol’ Stelios
to it?

00:07:13.150 --> 00:07:18.610
Oswald Bruce Cooper designed Cooper Black
having been commissioned by Barnhart Brothers

00:07:18.610 --> 00:07:25.889
&amp; Spindler, a type foundry looking for a new
typeface to entice advertisers. It’s a serif

00:07:25.889 --> 00:07:32.759
font that doesn’t look like one—its weight
(which in typography-land means thickness),

00:07:32.759 --> 00:07:40.520
might otherwise be imposing, but somehow appears
friendly. Its large letters are close in size

00:07:40.520 --> 00:07:47.449
to their upper-case equivalents, the rounded
serifs are easy to see even from a distance,

00:07:47.449 --> 00:07:53.860
and the small ‘counters’—the enclosed
spaces in letters like o, p, and a—give

00:07:53.860 --> 00:08:01.180
it an almost chubby look. It’s a font which
looks best from a distance, and so slapping

00:08:01.180 --> 00:08:06.330
it on the side of a massive passenger jet
is an obvious application.

00:08:06.330 --> 00:08:14.120
Albertus is another font where bigger is better,
but this time not for its chunkiness. It conveys

00:08:14.120 --> 00:08:20.289
a traditional roman style with a touch more
flair than most, and so it’s no surprise

00:08:20.289 --> 00:08:26.099
that it adorns countless classy nameplates
in the financial district of London.

00:08:26.099 --> 00:08:31.940
Albertus was created by Berthold Wolpe, a
Jewish German calligrapher who fled the Nazis

00:08:31.940 --> 00:08:38.560
to England in 1935. That’s the problem when
you’re a racist megalomaniac, you end up

00:08:38.560 --> 00:08:45.180
scaring away your best talent, handing the
enemy advantages like pretty typography and,

00:08:45.180 --> 00:08:52.570
you know, atomic bombs. Wolpe joined the famous
publishing house Faber and Faber to work on

00:08:52.570 --> 00:08:59.350
book jackets, featuring both established and
up and coming authors like TS Eliot, Seamus

00:08:59.350 --> 00:09:04.970
Heaney and William Golding.
Albertus was a font he built from scratch,

00:09:04.970 --> 00:09:09.930
drawing on his training in a bronze foundry,
where he’d carved raised inscriptions on

00:09:09.930 --> 00:09:16.320
memorial tablets. It was this process of physically
cutting away the metal from around the letters

00:09:16.320 --> 00:09:23.980
that led him to develop such a sharp, bold
font that was easy to read even from a distance,

00:09:23.980 --> 00:09:29.360
and with details which looked fantastic at
larger sizes, like the sharp point in the

00:09:29.360 --> 00:09:34.270
centre of its capital B.
If it looks familiar but you don’t ever

00:09:34.270 --> 00:09:40.600
recall seeing its large letters emblazoned
across a financial firm’s high-rise building,

00:09:40.600 --> 00:09:47.730
imagine, for a second, standing on the edge
of that skyscraper. Would you experience “a

00:09:47.730 --> 00:09:54.870
rush of blood to the head” or wish you had
“parachutes” with you? That’s right,

00:09:54.870 --> 00:10:00.580
Albertus was also coincidentally the font
of choice for the covers of Coldplay’s first

00:10:00.580 --> 00:10:04.520
three albums.
Albertus is an older font that still sees

00:10:04.520 --> 00:10:13.850
use today, but when things are the other way
around, trouble can arise. New fonts in places

00:10:13.850 --> 00:10:19.840
they shouldn’t be are a pet peeve of those
in the industry, especially when it comes

00:10:19.840 --> 00:10:27.980
to films. Used well, fonts can add realism
to scenes set in the past. But type designer

00:10:27.980 --> 00:10:34.630
Mark Simonson, whose most popular font, Proxima
Nova – which is used by sites like Buzzfeed,

00:10:34.630 --> 00:10:42.870
Mashable, and Wired – has a section of his
website dedicated to the use and misuse of

00:10:42.870 --> 00:10:48.500
certain fonts in movies.
Even well-loved and celebrated films like

00:10:48.500 --> 00:10:55.550
Chocolat, which garnered five Academy Award
nominations, aren’t spared. Set in rural

00:10:55.550 --> 00:11:01.170
1950s France, it’s hard to believe that
the town’s mayor could have produced a notice

00:11:01.170 --> 00:11:11.970
that uses ITC Benguiat, a typeface created
in 1978. While these details may sound minor,

00:11:11.970 --> 00:11:18.140
the few fun-loving type experts among us will
nevertheless appreciate the effort that’s

00:11:18.140 --> 00:11:22.990
gone into films that get it right. Oh, and
if you’re sitting there wondering what’s

00:11:22.990 --> 00:11:29.460
the damned difference between a typeface and
a font: a typeface refers to a font family

00:11:29.460 --> 00:11:37.520
like Helvetica, whereas a specific font is
a style of that family such as Helvetica Bold

00:11:37.520 --> 00:11:42.440
or Helvetica Light.
I’d wager that nowadays more people than

00:11:42.440 --> 00:11:48.280
ever before will recognise the fonts used
in films. People are becoming more aware of

00:11:48.280 --> 00:11:55.860
the fonts they use, because, well computers.
. Having a favourite font is no longer the

00:11:55.860 --> 00:12:02.350
preserve of typographers, it’s become another
way we can express ourselves, as a Cambria

00:12:02.350 --> 00:12:10.360
man or a Johnston Sans gal—the latter being
the corporate font commissioned and used by

00:12:10.360 --> 00:12:17.830
London public transport since 1913, and a
particular favourite of mine, which I suppose

00:12:17.830 --> 00:12:24.090
makes me a Johnston Sans gal.
Most of us don’t realise we care about fonts

00:12:24.090 --> 00:12:33.370
until we suddenly do. This was the case in
late 2009, after IKEA swapped all in-store

00:12:33.370 --> 00:12:40.990
signage from the slim and elegant Futura to
the modern Verdana. The reason for the switch

00:12:40.990 --> 00:12:46.720
was consistency—IKEA already used Verdana
on its website; the font was everywhere thanks

00:12:46.720 --> 00:12:53.320
to its adoption by Google and Microsoft. And
they’ve only gone and switched it yet again,

00:12:53.320 --> 00:12:59.870
in 2019, to Google’s Noto typeface.
Futura, on the other hand, is considered quirky

00:12:59.870 --> 00:13:06.500
and timeless, despite being heralded as “the
typeface of our time” way back in 1920s

00:13:06.500 --> 00:13:13.960
Germany, it still has a modern, innovative
feel to it— and is perhaps why it adorns

00:13:13.960 --> 00:13:20.930
not only Volkswagen adverts but also the inscribed
plaque which the Apollo 11 astronauts left

00:13:20.930 --> 00:13:25.160
on the moon.
While the lunar plaque is expected to stand

00:13:25.160 --> 00:13:32.380
the test of time, the same cannot be said
for what many consider to be the most beautiful

00:13:32.380 --> 00:13:39.960
font ever made. The Doves Press was founded
in the late 19th century by T.J. Cobden-Sanderson

00:13:39.960 --> 00:13:45.990
and Emery Walker and named after a nearby
riverside pub in Hammersmith. Doves Press

00:13:45.990 --> 00:13:53.780
created what many consider one of the most
beautiful fonts: known as Doves Roman, or

00:13:53.780 --> 00:14:02.670
simply Doves. The Doves Bible of 1902 is regarded
as a masterpiece, proudly displaying a g with

00:14:02.670 --> 00:14:11.530
a dynamic, tilted bowl and a sharp, definite
y whose tail is a straight slash. Sadly, we

00:14:11.530 --> 00:14:17.940
can no longer enjoy Doves in its original
glory after a dispute between Cobden-Sanderson

00:14:17.940 --> 00:14:25.171
and Walker saw much of the font lost forever.
Upon dissolving their partnership, a long

00:14:25.171 --> 00:14:32.850
and bitter legal dispute eventually saw Cobden-Sanderson
come away with the rights to Doves, but only

00:14:32.850 --> 00:14:39.590
until his death, after which Walker would
receive them. Cobden-Sanderson then reneged

00:14:39.590 --> 00:14:44.630
on the agreement. Worried by the thought that
his beautiful font would be used to print

00:14:44.630 --> 00:14:53.140
cheap publications or crude material, he spent
3 years slowly dumping the font—from the

00:14:53.140 --> 00:14:59.350
heavy casts for the physical letters to the
solid metal pages that had been used on their

00:14:59.350 --> 00:15:05.370
final book—into the Thames.
He’d take the blocks, wrap them in paper,

00:15:05.370 --> 00:15:11.450
and then carry them down the road, often walking
half a mile or more to find the best spot.

00:15:11.450 --> 00:15:16.380
There, he’d wait for heavy traffic so that
no one would hear the splash they’d make—he

00:15:16.380 --> 00:15:22.320
wrote of his fear that the police, the public,
even the newspapers would catch him in the

00:15:22.320 --> 00:15:31.381
act. This process took Cobden-Sanderson, then
in his 70s, some 6 months and 170 trips, but

00:15:31.381 --> 00:15:38.670
eventually Doves was no more. His crimes against
the typographic world were only discovered

00:15:38.670 --> 00:15:45.660
after his death, detailed as they were in
his will and his diary. Incensed, Walker promptly

00:15:45.660 --> 00:15:52.790
opened legal proceedings against the widowed
Anne Cobden-Sanderson, who eventually settled

00:15:52.790 --> 00:15:56.730
for £700, equivalent to tens of thousands
today.

00:15:56.730 --> 00:16:03.720
Thankfully, Doves isn’t completely lost.
Swedish designer Torbjörn Olsson created

00:16:03.720 --> 00:16:10.950
a digital revival of the font in 1994, attempting
to emulate the rounded corners and imperfections

00:16:10.950 --> 00:16:18.080
that gave the printed characters so much,
well, character. Another designer, Robert

00:16:18.080 --> 00:16:24.681
Green, made an even more polished version
of Doves in 2013, and two years later, he

00:16:24.681 --> 00:16:30.390
enlisted help from the Port of London Authority
to search the riverbed near Hammersmith Bridge

00:16:30.390 --> 00:16:39.430
and recover 150 pieces of Cobden-Sanderson’s
original type, further improving the accuracy

00:16:39.430 --> 00:16:46.120
of his recreation. Today, we’re once again
able to enjoy Doves almost exactly as its

00:16:46.120 --> 00:16:51.230
creator intended—just don’t use it to
print salacious letters to your neighbourhood

00:16:51.230 --> 00:16:58.923
strumpet or his ghost will doubtless appear
to throw you into the Thames as well.

00:16:58.923 --> 00:17:03.910
The neater style that Doves and countless
others followed was first popularised in the

00:17:03.910 --> 00:17:11.740
16th century. Claude Garamond made several
fonts in the 16th century for Francis I king

00:17:11.740 --> 00:17:19.049
of – you’ll never guess! – France Both
the Dr Suess books and the US editions of

00:17:19.049 --> 00:17:26.409
Harry Potter use Garamond. While the font
may look archaic to us, it marked a move away

00:17:26.409 --> 00:17:33.490
from heavy, intricate gothic letters to the
Roman alphabet we use today. Despite the far

00:17:33.490 --> 00:17:39.899
cleaner lines he introduced, Garamond allowed
himself more artistic expression with one

00:17:39.899 --> 00:17:49.720
particular character: the ampersand. Originally
a joining of e and t from the Latin word for

00:17:49.720 --> 00:17:58.100
and, ‘et’, ampersands gradually took on
a life of their own, and Garamond’s version

00:17:58.100 --> 00:18:05.549
is a calligraphic masterpiece, with its elegant
curls and tapering lines, although it must’ve

00:18:05.549 --> 00:18:11.950
been a bitch to cut out of metal.
The ampersand itself isn’t merely an abbreviation—it

00:18:11.950 --> 00:18:19.340
has the added effect of bringing the conjoined
elements closer together. Phrases like R&amp;B

00:18:19.340 --> 00:18:24.640
look far more compact and ‘edgier’ with
only three characters. It makes its way into

00:18:24.640 --> 00:18:32.019
business names like AT&amp;T, Johnson &amp; Johnson,
and Dolce &amp; Gabbana, offering an intimacy

00:18:32.019 --> 00:18:38.840
that the word “and” cannot.
The symbol has such emotive potential that,

00:18:38.840 --> 00:18:48.149
in 2010, the Society of Typographic Aficionados
released Coming Together, a $20 purchasable

00:18:48.149 --> 00:18:55.629
font consisting entirely of over 400 different
ampersands drawn by designers from across

00:18:55.629 --> 00:19:00.429
the world to raise money for the victims of
the Haiti earthquake.

00:19:00.429 --> 00:19:08.080
The huge emotive potential of fonts, however,
can be used less altruistically to influence

00:19:08.080 --> 00:19:15.840
our minds. When Barack Obama ran for the US
presidency in 2008 his team chose the modern,

00:19:15.840 --> 00:19:22.809
sleek and streamlined font Gotham.
Developed in 2000 by Tobias Frere-Jones for

00:19:22.809 --> 00:19:29.771
GQ magazine, it took inspiration from signs
around New York City, particularly the 3D

00:19:29.771 --> 00:19:36.410
lettering that adorned the city’s Port Authority
Bus terminal. Frere-Jones, seeing that many

00:19:36.410 --> 00:19:41.990
of these physical signs were being replaced
by digital ones, took it upon himself to photograph

00:19:41.990 --> 00:19:49.610
3600 signs across the city, and these became
his muses.

00:19:49.610 --> 00:19:56.730
The simple, solid font that emerged was a
perfect fit for Obama’s campaign, ostensibly

00:19:56.730 --> 00:20:03.399
based on change and truth with a touch of
sophistication—picture the “Yes we can”

00:20:03.399 --> 00:20:09.039
slogan and it’s likely in robust Gotham.
It certainly went down better than his Democratic

00:20:09.039 --> 00:20:16.080
Party rival Hillary Clinton’s choice, with
her New Baskerville Bold, intended to lend

00:20:16.080 --> 00:20:22.350
a stately, legal quality to her name, but
instead publically squibed as something that

00:20:22.350 --> 00:20:28.919
“might have come off a heart-healthy cereal
box or a mildly embarrassing ointment.”

00:20:28.919 --> 00:20:35.600
Perhaps Hillary could’ve used some advice
from psychologist Aric Sigman. In 2001, printer

00:20:35.600 --> 00:20:43.120
company Lexmark commissioned Sigman to look
into what font choices revealed about their

00:20:43.120 --> 00:20:50.110
users. It was perhaps unsurprising that soft,
curvy fonts like Shelley were generally favoured

00:20:50.110 --> 00:20:56.499
by women, though Lexmark’s decision to brand
anyone using these fonts a “sex kitten”

00:20:56.499 --> 00:21:03.270
(yes, really) was pretty bold.
Lexmark also proclaimed Courier was the font

00:21:03.270 --> 00:21:10.190
of nerds, apparently favoured by librarians
and data entry companies—given being a faux

00:21:10.190 --> 00:21:15.970
nerd is far cooler now than it was 19 years
ago, perhaps it’ll soon be repurposed by

00:21:15.970 --> 00:21:21.470
the hipster hordes. Sans serif fonts were
chosen by those that valued their anonymity

00:21:21.470 --> 00:21:22.809
and safety, though that very conclusion seems
at odds with the logic that these neutral

00:21:22.809 --> 00:21:25.710
fonts don’t give too much away.
And of course, the study had to mention Comic

00:21:25.710 --> 00:21:32.730
Sans, with Lexmark merely branding anyone
that used it an attention seeker. Better that

00:21:32.730 --> 00:21:38.919
than the verdict from comic book artist Dave
Gibbons, whose work had initially inspired

00:21:38.919 --> 00:21:47.270
it, who called it a “real mess” and “a
particularly ugly letter form.” But why

00:21:47.270 --> 00:21:54.830
is Comic Sans so hated?
It was invented for Microsoft’s 1994 user-friendly

00:21:54.830 --> 00:22:02.289
home software package Bob. Bob included a
word processor and a finance manager, as well

00:22:02.289 --> 00:22:08.260
as a cute talking dog who assisted users,
but it also gave its instructions in Times

00:22:08.260 --> 00:22:16.320
New Roman. A hugely influential font,commissioned
in 1931 to make The Times newspaper easier

00:22:16.320 --> 00:22:22.230
to read. But Times New Roman was a little
too formal for Bob.

00:22:22.230 --> 00:22:28.350
Comic Sans was so named because it was inspired
by comics—its creator Vincent Connare read

00:22:28.350 --> 00:22:35.659
Batman and Watchmen comics and created comic
sans to be intentionally child-like with a

00:22:35.659 --> 00:22:41.580
hand-drawn feel, as if its rounded letters
had been carefully written one by one in the

00:22:41.580 --> 00:22:49.210
panels of a comic strip. Sans, from the French
‘without’ is from the phrase ‘sans serif’

00:22:49.210 --> 00:22:56.210
– a font without serifs, which are the finishing
strokes at the feet and tips of letters, which

00:22:56.210 --> 00:23:02.129
give type a more traditional feel.
In the end, Comic Sans wasn’t used in Bob,

00:23:02.129 --> 00:23:07.150
which may be why you’ve never heard of the
software – it flopped. Instead, it found

00:23:07.150 --> 00:23:14.580
its way into Microsoft 3D Movie Maker, released
in 1995, which allowed children to create

00:23:14.580 --> 00:23:21.250
choppy and goofy films with the help of a
frankly terrifying purple assistant called

00:23:21.250 --> 00:23:28.270
McZee. Despite McZee’s horrific appearance
and the program’s surplus of controls, it

00:23:28.270 --> 00:23:34.390
was a huge success and still has a dedicated
fanbase today. It was also responsible for

00:23:34.390 --> 00:23:40.259
unleashing Comic Sans on the world.
The font’s inclusion in Windows 95 further

00:23:40.259 --> 00:23:47.700
increased its reach—now people not only
saw it but used it. Its carefree appearance

00:23:47.700 --> 00:23:55.010
seemed perfect for home-printed birthday cards,
restaurant menus, and student essays. But

00:23:55.010 --> 00:24:01.820
then it got out of hand. It found its way
onto ambulances, textbooks, even the playing

00:24:01.820 --> 00:24:05.409
kit of the Portuguese national basketball
team.

00:24:05.409 --> 00:24:12.070
Two graphic designers, Dave and Holly Combs,
started the ‘Ban Comic Sans’ movement

00:24:12.070 --> 00:24:19.259
in 1999. They created their website after
being asked by an employer to use Comic Sans

00:24:19.259 --> 00:24:24.779
for a children’s museum exhibit. The pair
claim to have seen the font adorn horrendously

00:24:24.779 --> 00:24:36.149
unsuited places such as a gravestone and a
doctor’s leaflet on irritable bowel syndrome.

00:24:36.149 --> 00:24:46.029
But Comic Sans isn’t the monster some people
make it out to be either. It’s highly acclaimed

00:24:46.029 --> 00:24:52.409
for use with dyslexics, thanks to its simple,
clear letters, and it has featured in some

00:24:52.409 --> 00:24:58.409
well-loved places, like the credits of Sesame
Street in the early 21st century or at Disney

00:24:58.409 --> 00:25:02.450
theme parks.
Still, the big problem with Comic Sans is

00:25:02.450 --> 00:25:10.179
undoubtedly its use in all the wrong places.
So in the full report that Lexmark had commissioned,

00:25:10.179 --> 00:25:18.390
Sigman went on to detail the most suitable
fonts for certain situations. Romance calls

00:25:18.390 --> 00:25:26.420
for fonts with big, round Os which mimic human
faces and come across as more friendly. But

00:25:26.420 --> 00:25:32.419
if choosing a human-like font gets you into
a relationship, what about getting out of

00:25:32.419 --> 00:25:40.059
one? Here, you want something clear without
being harsh, perhaps an old faithful like

00:25:40.059 --> 00:25:47.100
Times Regular. Lighter, more optimistic fonts
that still feel respectful could be Verdana,

00:25:47.100 --> 00:25:54.480
if you need a firmer, more final end, however,
then a more impersonal Courier might be the

00:25:54.480 --> 00:25:58.340
best choice.
More formal circumstances require similar

00:25:58.340 --> 00:26:04.100
considerations, with a Verdana a fine choice
for a resignation letter from a job you enjoyed—it

00:26:04.100 --> 00:26:13.900
may not have been popular for IKEA, but it’s
a simple professional choice—but if you’re

00:26:13.900 --> 00:26:24.679
parting ways more acrimoniously, Sigman recommends
giving them the typographic equivalent of

00:26:24.679 --> 00:26:32.850
the middle finger: Arial. Ubiquitous and yet
unloved, Arial is despised by those in the

00:26:32.850 --> 00:26:38.549
know—whatever the opposite of a cult classic
is, Arial fits the bill.

00:26:38.549 --> 00:26:45.899
It was designed in the 1980s as a rip-off
of—sorry, “alternative to”—Helvetica,

00:26:45.899 --> 00:26:54.049
with which its spacing made it perfectly interchangeable.
Any document written in Helvetica could be

00:26:54.049 --> 00:27:01.110
changed to Arial and keep exactly the same
layout and size. Add in the fact that Arial’s

00:27:01.110 --> 00:27:06.309
creators then undercut Helvetica’s pricing
and you suddenly had Arial as standard on

00:27:06.309 --> 00:27:12.710
every Windows computer. The vast majority
of people don’t care and millions use Arial

00:27:12.710 --> 00:27:20.429
every single day. But if you know, you know,
and you use that knowledge to subtly tell

00:27:20.429 --> 00:27:28.549
that former boss where he can shove his terrible
job by giving him the Arial treatment.

00:27:28.549 --> 00:27:34.929
So maybe Arial has its place too. After all,
what better use is there for a font than a

00:27:34.929 --> 00:27:48.090
passive aggressive email?

